The mechanism, verified:

```js
// THE CHAIN — property access walks up until found or null
const animal = { eat() { return 'eating'; } };
const dog = Object.create(animal);         // dog's [[Prototype]] = animal
dog.eat();        // 'eating' — found on animal via the chain ✓
dog.hasOwnProperty('eat');   // false — it's on the PROTOTYPE ✓
dog.bark = () => 'woof';      // own property — shadows nothing

// THE CONSTRUCTOR PATTERN (what pre-class code looked like)
function Cat(name) { this.name = name; }       // per-INSTANCE data
Cat.prototype.meow = function() { return this.name + ' meows'; };  // SHARED method
const c = new Cat('Mia');
c.meow();                        // 'Mia meows' — method found on Cat.prototype ✓
Object.getPrototypeOf(c) === Cat.prototype;   // true ✓
// chain: c → Cat.prototype → Object.prototype → null

// CLASS = THE SAME THING, SWEETER
// CLASS = THE SAME THING, SWEETER
class Rabbit {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }   // own data per instance
  hop() { return this.name + ' hops'; }     // → Rabbit.prototype.hop ✓
}
const r = new Rabbit('Bun');
r.hasOwnProperty('hop');  // false — prototype method ✓ (verified)
```

Why methods live on the prototype (the design question):
- ONE function object shared by all instances — memory: 1000 instances, 1 meow
- vs. putting methods in the constructor (this.meow = ...) — 1000 copies
- This is why hasOwnProperty('hop') is false: the method belongs to the CLASS, not the instance — and it's why inheritance is CHEAP: subclassing links prototypes, it doesn't copy anything

The chain-lookup contract (the mental model to state): `obj.x` → own? → proto.x? → proto.proto.x? → ... → null → undefined. Assignment (obj.x = v) creates an OWN property (it never walks the chain to write — that's why setting a property doesn't affect other instances).

